            <ArticleTitle>Effect of intracoronary balloon inflation at percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty on QT dispersion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>519-22</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">QT dispersion (QTd) has recently been used as a marker for arrhythmic death caused by adverse drug effect. However, the effect of acute ischemia on QTd is not well described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">To determine the effect of acute ischemia on QTd, 12-lead electrocardiograms were obtained at baseline and 2 minutes after intracoronary balloon inflation in 34 patients. QTd was calculated as a range between shortest (QTmin) and longest (QTmax) QT intervals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In 18 patients balloon inflation provoked ST elevation, increased QTd (43 +/- 20 msec to 61 +/- 19 msec, p = 0.012), and decreased QTmin (390 +/- 43 msec to 361 +/- 43 msec, p &lt; 0.001) without significant effect on QTmax (433 +/- 42 msec to 423 +/- 47 msec). No significant changes in QT measures were observed in eight patients who had ST depression and in eight without ST changes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Acute transmural ischemia provoked by intracoronary balloon inflation increases QTd by reducing the QTmin interval, which may modify the electrophysiologic substrate.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
